WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Historically, React Native Execution Environments operated as entirely isolated landmasses,

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.480
Android Native Code on one side, and JavaScript on the other.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.600
To bridge this gap, the architecture relied on an asynchronous message passing system.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.120
Functionally, this operated as a highly inefficient inter-process communication pipeline.

5
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.440
The new architecture rewrites this execution model by completely eliminating the asynchronous messaging queue.

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.320
This schematic illustrates the replacement structure.

7
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.960
The old queue is gone, replaced by a solid C++ layer called the JavaScript Interface, or JSI.

8
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.400
To understand exactly how this layer bypasses the old JSON serialization bottlenecks,

9
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.360
we have to map its execution model directly against standard Android thread concepts.

10
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.000
From there, we can break down the mechanics of transferring large data blocks between these environments

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.520
with zero copying.

12
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.280
By stripping out the messaging queue, React Native moves away from isolated message passing,

13
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.720
and toward a system of direct interaction between environments.

14
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.960
In the legacy pipeline, every cross-environment call is forced into a serialization block

15
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.840
and converted to JSON. The payload sits in an asynchronous queue, piling up.

16
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.720
Finally, Android must fully deserialize that string back into native objects before execution begins.

17
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.120
This imposes a severe execution penalty. It operates exactly like an Android looper that

18
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.040
strictly accepts post commands, but never posts sync. You simply cannot block the JavaScript thread

19
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.120
to wait for a native result. The compute cost is equally restrictive.

20
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.120
Forcing every cross-environment call through JSON formatting

21
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.600
incurs the same CQ and memory overhead as serializing every single binder IPC call to a text string

22
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.640
before dispatching it. Because these two execution environments operate in isolation,

23
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:58.080
they completely lack a shared memory map. Passing high throughput data across this boundary,

24
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.760
like an uncompressed camera frame, requires allocating new memory and physically duplicating the

25
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.760
entire buffer, byte for byte. The old bridge failed at high performance tasks because it forced

26
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.080
two blind processes to construct and decode text messages rather than sharing a common resource pool.

27
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.040
JSI introduces a native C++ layer that slides directly between the Hermes JavaScript engine

28
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.240
and native Android. Conceptually, this operates exactly like the Java native interface.

29
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.920
Where JNI bridges the execution gap between Kotlin and C++, JSI bridges the gap between JavaScript

30
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.160
and C++. When JavaScript calls a native method, it triggers a direct C++ function call.

31
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.440
This works because the Hermes engine now holds a direct pointer reference to the C++

32
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.120
object residing in memory. By holding this direct reference, the architecture removes the latency

33
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.000
of the bridge, bringing cross-environment calls significantly closer to the speed of standard

34
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.400
native function execution. This specific C++ block is called a host object. This is the functional

35
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.160
primitive that JSI exposes to the JavaScript engine. JavaScript interacts with these native C++

36
00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.440
classes exactly as if they were standard local J objects, allowing for instantaneous synchronous

37
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.280
data returns. Modern libraries like the key value storage solution MMKV rely on this exact

38
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.360
architecture. By utilizing JSI directly, MMKV bypasses the traditional bridge entirely to execute

39
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.480
database reads with zero asynchronous delay. Integrating host objects removes the architectural

40
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.120
requirement to build loading states or asynchronous wrappers when a component fetches native data.

41
00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.680
Instead of physically duplicating a data block into a new memory pool, both environments now look

42
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:48.400
at the exact same block. This is achieved for the shared array buffer. During execution, no actual

43
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.480
data moves across the boundary, only the reference pointer is passed. This direct referencing solves

44
00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.160
bottlenecks for real-time camera processing, audio buffers, and continuous high throughput data streams.

45
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.760
Shifting from memory duplication to memory referencing provides the sustained bandwidth

46
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.280
necessary for hardware intensive mobile features, like real-time computer vision or high-fidelity audio

47
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:17.680
processing. This visualization shows the new architecture runtime stack. The system now operates

48
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:24.640
across distinct JS, UI, and background threads, all interconnected by the JSI layer. This thread

49
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.680
separation allows the new native rendering engine fabric to achieve true concurrent rendering.

50
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.200
This execution model closely mirrors Android's native split between the render thread and the UI

51
00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.960
thread. Because layout calculations are handled natively, low priority state updates can yield their

52
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.200
execution time, preventing long tasks from blocking the UI thread. By decoupling UI rendering from

53
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.400
JavaScript execution blocking, the application maintains native level frame rates even during heavy

54
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:59.440
data processing. In practice, developers rarely write raw JSI C++ code to achieve this performance.

55
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.320
The modern workflow starts by writing a TypeScript spec, which establishes the precise architectural

56
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.840
contract for the module. A tool called CodeGen meets that specification and automatically generates the

57
00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.680
necessary C++ JSI glue layer. The final step is simply writing the Kotlin implementation to fulfill

58
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:22.000
that generated contract. JSI transitions React Native to a model of direct synchronous memory access.

59
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.040
The next step is utilizing this workflow to build a functional Kotlin turbo module, entirely free

60
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.040
from bridge over Hype.

